WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.276 --> 00:00:03.490
(upbeat electronic music)

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:05.680
- Welcome to Ear Biscuits, I'm Link.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.513
- And I'm Rhett.

00:00:06.513 --> 00:00:08.710
This week at the round
table of dim lighting,

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:12.140
we are exploring the
question how do we deal

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:15.770
with a medical crisis and
let me just say first of all,

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:16.880
we're both fine.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.690
Neither one of us are
personally in a medical crisis.

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:21.940
- Right.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:22.773
But--
- This is something

00:00:22.773 --> 00:00:25.980
that happened to Link--
- Over the past week.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.030
- Not personally but, personally but--

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:32.080
- I have been dealing with
a family medical crisis

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:37.080
which I'd love to just be able
to verbally process through

00:00:39.238 --> 00:00:43.180
with you, gentle listener, as
well as you, gentle friend.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:44.013
- And I don't wanna--
- Gentleman.

00:00:44.013 --> 00:00:46.930
- I'm not trying to spoil anything here.

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:48.010
- But we should--

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:49.610
- This person that you're gonna be talking

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:52.140
about is fine now.
- Yes.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.770
- 'Cause we're gonna be having fun.

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:58.000
This is gonna be also light-hearted.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.270
- Yeah.
- We're gonna talk about

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:01.700
some serious things that
happened but we're also gonna be

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:05.120
having a good time joking
because in the end,

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:06.490
everything is okay.

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:09.040
Well this is what happened

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.830
and I'll walk through the
details and unpack it.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:14.913
- Well first we gotta let people know,

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.760
we're gonna keep telling you--

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.500
- Oh about our dates.
- Educating you about

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:20.620
where we're going to be.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:23.010
We are coming to a town
near you if you happen

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:27.000
to be in Las Vegas, we're
gonna be there on June 21st,

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.340
Salt Lake City June 22nd, Denver the 23rd,

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:33.290
Milwaukee the 25th, Indianapolis the 26th,

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:36.470
Detroit the 27th, Omaha the 29th,

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:39.180
and Minneapolis on the 30th.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:41.840
Again this is Rhett and
Link live in concert,

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:43.680
not the Tour of Mythicality

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.140
and debuting, well, if you've seen 'em,

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:50.640
there's some new songs that
have never been heard before

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.700
on the internets and then
of course the old classics,

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.440
the rap battles, there's
potential dress-up that happens.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.350
- Dress-up, that is a good way to put it.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:02.160
- Anyway, it's--
- We play dress-up.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.230
You buy a ticket, we play dress-up.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:07.410
- And there's also VIP tickets available.

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:08.990
Of course you're all
very important people,

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:13.310
that's just the industry
vernacular to describe someone

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.610
who pays for a ticket where
we get to have a little meet

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:19.610
and greet so all that and
more at RhettAndLinkLive.com.

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:21.920
- Come see us, RhettAndLinkLive.com.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:26.920
So in some ways I am
still very much processing

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:30.003
what happened over the past week.

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:36.150
And it involves my mom's husband Lewis

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:38.543
who I've talked about many times.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.750
He's technically my step-dad
because he's my mom's husband

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:45.800
but he's not my dad.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:47.050
- He's your step-dad.

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:51.580
- That's what I just said but
I've never actually ended up

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:53.420
calling him my step-dad.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:56.550
I'll end up saying something
like he's my father-in-law

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:58.680
if I'm pressed.
- Which is incorrect.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:00.540
- But because--
- And strange.

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:03.360
- He married my mom, I
mean they were dating

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:06.703
when I was in high school, you know,

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:10.660
I've known him for a
long time and I love him.

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:12.070
- But he didn't raise you.

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:14.900
- I do love him but he didn't raise me

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.670
and I never lived with him.

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:20.210
So I got married and
then Christy was pregnant

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:23.270
with Lily and that's when my
mom and Lewis got married,

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:26.410
so there's an interesting
dynamic to the nature

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:29.730
of our relationship that
I love him, but he's not,

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:31.620
he is my step-dad but
I don't call him that,

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.990
in the experience it
just doesn't feel like

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:36.320
what a step-dad feels like.
- And actually I think that

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:39.320
my mom's husband is the correct--

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:41.620
- Well that's true too.
- No I know but--

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:43.820
- Father-in-law.
- If I meet a--

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:45.870
- Is not correct.
- If I meet a person,

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:48.050
if I'm just at a party and
I'm having a conversation

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:49.430
with somebody and they
begin telling me a story

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:51.870
and they say my mom's
husband, I immediately know,

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:53.840
oh not your step-dad,
your step-dad is somebody

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:55.940
who you usually would
say raised you, right,

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:58.440
but father-in-law, you
gotta stop saying that.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:03:59.405
- But father-in-law--

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:01.990
- 'Cause what's gonna happen
is you're gonna be seen

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:05.440
with him in public at some
point and they're gonna be like

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:07.640
oh that's your father-in-law,
no this is my father-in-law.

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:09.740
Then you're one of those
guys that has two families,

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:11.890
one being secret.
(Link sputters)

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:14.310
And you can't be one of those guys.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:16.700
- Well, I mean I do have a secret family

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:18.260
but I have nothing to do with this.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:21.730
- I will say that Shepherd asked me...

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:26.960
There was something in the car

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:29.987
and when he got into the car
the other day and he was like,

00:04:29.987 --> 00:04:30.820
"Where's this from?"

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:34.727
I said, "Oh that's, one of
the kids in my secret family

00:04:34.727 --> 00:04:36.580
"left it in here." (chuckling)

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:38.840
I just couldn't help myself.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:41.690
- Hopefully you meant imaginary family.

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:42.537
- I said, "You know Locke,

00:04:42.537 --> 00:04:44.910
"a lot of people have a second family."

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:45.743
- So don't joke about it.

00:04:45.743 --> 00:04:47.040
Oh I thought you said this was Shepherd.

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:48.690
You said Locke.
- It was Shepherd.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:51.100
- Oh you just said Locke,
you meant Shepherd.

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:53.290
- Okay I meant Shepherd
the whole time, anyway,

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:54.820
I joke like that with my children.

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:58.010
They know that it's a
joke so get off my case.

00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:01.130
- But was he joking when he
told you is the question?

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:01.963
- He didn't tell me.

00:05:01.963 --> 00:05:03.650
He said, "Where's this from?"
and I said, "It's from--"

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:04.613
- Oh you said that.

00:05:04.613 --> 00:05:06.540
I messed up.
- I think everyone else

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:08.910
understood, I don't know if
I said it in a confusing way

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:11.670
but I'm sorry if I did.
- So confused.

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:15.150
So anyway, Lewis, my father-in-law.

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:16.340
(chuckles)

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:18.740
That does come into the
story later, by the way.

00:05:21.416 --> 00:05:26.150
He's doing well now but he had,

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:30.403
I don't wanna
over-sensationalize it but...

00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:33.790
- Well then just sensationalize it.

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:35.810
- I'll just say but I do think

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:38.363
it was a brush with mortality.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:40.603
Brush with death.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.100
It took some time for that to sink in

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:46.790
and so I'll unpack that, like I said,

00:05:46.790 --> 00:05:48.930
some of this I'm still processing

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:52.300
but he gave me permission
to talk about it here

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:55.773
and take it for what it's worth.

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:01.740
My mom and Lewis, they
came out here last year

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:04.020
and then we visited for Thanksgiving

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:05.480
and then that's the last
time we've seen them

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:08.143
in person so they kinda
wanted to come out again.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:14.810
But they've got a house,
they've got farmland.

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:17.860
He drives around his farmland on his Gator

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:20.790
and he surveys his land
and he sees turkeys

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:23.669
and he keeps an eye on how
many turkeys will come around.

00:06:23.669 --> 00:06:24.583
- And for those of you
who don't understand

00:06:24.583 --> 00:06:28.010
what a Gator is, it doesn't
mean he's on an alligator.

00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:31.790
He's on, it's like a John Deere

00:06:31.790 --> 00:06:34.550
crazy off-road golf cart kinda thing.

00:06:34.550 --> 00:06:36.410
- I kinda wanted the mystique to be that

00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:39.330
maybe he's just saddled
an alligator but fine.

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:40.390
- Well if you wanna believe
that, yeah, go ahead.

00:06:40.390 --> 00:06:42.220
- Clear up the mystique.

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:43.870
He's got cows.

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:47.210
Mom named some of them and
then he takes care of 'em.

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:48.210
They have babies,

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:50.970
they make sure that
everything's good to go.

00:06:50.970 --> 00:06:52.720
Basically he has a farm to run.

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:54.740
- Right.
- So it's hard to get away

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:58.133
and they're not the traveling type.

00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:01.841
So we planned this a long time in advance

00:07:01.841 --> 00:07:05.900
and I think Mom came down with something,

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:07.540
they had to reschedule one time.

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:09.150
Something came up, I can't remember.

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:12.290
But then weekend before
last they were coming out

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:14.250
and they were gonna show up Friday night

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:18.120
and then they like to stay
in a hotel, not at our house

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:20.900
so we make 'em feel comfortable
whatever way they want

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:23.450
and then they were gonna fly back Monday.

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:24.720
- That's a quick trip.
- They were only staying

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:25.940
two days.
- Those cows.

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:27.100
- They gotta get back to their cows.

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:28.277
- Somebody's gotta look after 'em.

00:07:28.277 --> 00:07:30.500
- And we were very much looking
forward to making the most

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:31.800
of those two days.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:33.210
So we pick 'em up at the airport,

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:36.450
the whole family's in the car
and we're crammed into there

00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:37.850
and we pick 'em up at the curb

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:40.950
and I can immediately tell that

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:48.570
Lewis is road ragged,
he doesn't look great.

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:50.960
I'm like man the flight
must not have been great.

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:52.410
They get in the car, put the luggage in

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:54.410
and we're driving them, it was late so

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:55.510
especially by their time

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:58.340
so we just went straight to their hotel.

00:07:58.340 --> 00:07:59.360
- Did you say anything at that point?

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:01.940
Did you say, "You look awful."

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:03.233
You shouldn't say that to someone right

00:08:03.233 --> 00:08:04.648
when they get off of a plane.

00:08:04.648 --> 00:08:05.940
I've made that mistake.

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:08.037
- Did not look good but I was like,

00:08:08.037 --> 00:08:09.080
"Well how was the flight?"

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:12.160
And they said it was okay.
- A lot of turbulence?

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:14.220
- Lewis didn't have much appetite.

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:16.530
They didn't say much about
turbulence or anything like that

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:21.000
but he was very quiet and
you may have heard me talk

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.570
about this before but the thing
we love to talk about Lewis

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:25.603
is that he's got this catchphrase.

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:29.874
Good, good, good, like if
he's talking to the kids,

00:08:29.874 --> 00:08:31.680
the whole week leading up to them coming,

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:33.670
I'm talking to all the kids and I'm like,

00:08:33.670 --> 00:08:36.640
I'm talking like Lewis, I'm
doing my impersonation of him,

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:39.700
like Lincoln, you been
doing your push-ups?

00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:41.100
How's basketball?

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:42.980
And I'm like Lincoln, you're
not playing basketball anymore

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:44.260
so you gotta be ready for your answer

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:46.340
'cause you know Papa's gonna
ask you about basketball

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:47.493
and your push-ups.
- You might to just make up--

00:08:47.493 --> 00:08:48.726
- [Link] You've not been doing either.

00:08:48.726 --> 00:08:50.300
- [Rhett] That you're
still playing basketball.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:53.390
- So he has his answer
prepared and I was like,

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:54.668
you can just tell him that
you're mountain biking

00:08:54.668 --> 00:08:56.640
with your dad and you're
still staying active

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:59.181
and then he'll just
say, "Good, good, good."

00:08:59.181 --> 00:09:01.323
- (scoffs) Giving your kids answers.

00:09:01.323 --> 00:09:02.855
- (chuckles) Just trying to coach--

00:09:02.855 --> 00:09:05.430
- Just let them come up
with their own answers, man.

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:07.400
- But we were looking
forward to that catchphrase

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:09.620
and I couldn't help
but notice he was quiet

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:11.560
and there was no good,
good, goods happening

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:14.650
on the ride to the hotel.

00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:15.870
- But he wasn't saying, "Bad, bad, bad."

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:17.951
- He was not saying--
- It doesn't go that far.

00:09:17.951 --> 00:09:21.020
- No and I took a wrong turn and me

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:24.710
and Christy kinda snapped
at each other a little bit.

00:09:24.710 --> 00:09:25.950
- What about the GPS?

00:09:25.950 --> 00:09:28.560
- Well you know how when
sometimes your phone's hooked up

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:31.810
to the cords and then
the map's not showing up

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:35.985
on the car play and I'm having to drive

00:09:35.985 --> 00:09:38.140
but I can't see the turn,

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:40.600
and now I'm going the wrong
way and I'm getting angry

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:42.560
and I gotta direct that anger at somebody

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:44.370
even though my mama's in the
car and I haven't seen her

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:45.560
in person since Thanksgiving.

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:48.040
I'm not gonna direct it
towards my mom or Lewis.

00:09:48.040 --> 00:09:49.340
So Christy got a little bit of that,

00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:51.178
she gave it right back.
- Good.

00:09:51.178 --> 00:09:53.310
Good, good, good.
- Good, good, good.

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:56.610
I deserved it and then so
I thought that the silence

00:09:56.610 --> 00:10:00.990
might have been as a
result of me being an.

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:03.210
- Right, okay.

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:06.460
- We get to the hotel, we
check 'em in, they're tired.

00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:09.310
We let 'em go to sleep, I was like,

00:10:09.310 --> 00:10:11.870
we'll come pick you up as early
as you want in the morning.

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:13.660
You're on east coast time.

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:16.669
Well, Mom never called
so we finally called

00:10:16.669 --> 00:10:21.010
by mid-morning and she's
like Lewis is not doing well.

00:10:21.010 --> 00:10:26.010
He's feeling faint and he's passing blood

00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:28.750
when he uses the bathroom.

00:10:28.750 --> 00:10:29.833
And I'm like well,

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:33.570
not good, good, good.
- No, mm-mm.

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:35.670
- So we come, we pick
'em up and we're like,

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:38.130
we go straight to the emergency room.

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:41.320
- Now explain that
decision to me because--

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:43.010
- Well when you're passing blood--

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:44.150
- Well no no--
- You need to go

00:10:44.150 --> 00:10:46.772
to the emergency room.
- I'm telling you,

00:10:46.772 --> 00:10:51.772
I am the kind of person
that always minimizes.

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:54.670
You know what I'm saying--
- I'm that way too.

00:10:54.670 --> 00:10:56.580
This was Christy driving this train.

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:59.930
- Right so Christy, she's
good in those situations.

00:10:59.930 --> 00:11:03.930
She has a natural nursing instinct.

00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:05.457
I've never nursed her.

00:11:05.457 --> 00:11:07.268
(Link sputters)
Just so you understand.

00:11:07.268 --> 00:11:08.101
- Great.

00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:13.850
- But she has that, she's
good in medical situations.

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:15.990
There's a certain mode--

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:20.890
- She goes into this
ready contingency mode.

00:11:20.890 --> 00:11:22.380
When we were going to
sleep the night before,

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:26.680
she was like, now the nearest
hospital where they're staying

00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:30.390
in Pasadena is the Huntington Hospital.

00:11:30.390 --> 00:11:33.660
- For those of you who
understand the Enneagram,

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:35.730
a six is really well-equipped--

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:37.910
- That's what she is.
- At these sort of

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:38.810
contingency plans.

00:11:38.810 --> 00:11:42.590
- So she was like I think
Lewis wasn't doing good

00:11:42.590 --> 00:11:45.840
and we may have to take
him to the emergency room.

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:46.713
She already said that the night before

00:11:46.713 --> 00:11:48.873
and that's why she was calling Mom.

00:11:51.093 --> 00:11:55.433
They checked him in and he was...

00:11:57.060 --> 00:11:59.050
I actually don't think
he passed more blood

00:11:59.050 --> 00:12:01.380
when he was there and then
it takes awhile to process

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:03.670
and they said we're gonna admit you

00:12:03.670 --> 00:12:06.640
because something's going on
here, there's internal bleeding

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:09.417
or there's evidence of that.

00:12:09.417 --> 00:12:11.113
And your blood count is low.

00:12:12.130 --> 00:12:17.130
They check him in to the
ICU, intensive care unit,

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:20.840
and so they're monitoring him very closely

00:12:20.840 --> 00:12:23.837
and one of the nurses said to Mom,

00:12:23.837 --> 00:12:26.537
"Your husband is very sick."

00:12:27.652 --> 00:12:30.320
I was like well what does that mean?

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:31.283
We don't know yet.

00:12:32.928 --> 00:12:36.957
The gastro doctor comes
in and they did a scope

00:12:38.910 --> 00:12:41.600
from down his throat--
- Oh man.

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:43.810
- They put him under for a little bit,

00:12:43.810 --> 00:12:45.220
we stepped out of the room

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:50.270
and less than a half hour
they did this procedure

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:52.500
where they put the
scope down his esophagus

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:54.700
through his stomach, looking at everything

00:12:54.700 --> 00:12:58.220
into his small intestine and
then we come back in there

00:12:58.220 --> 00:13:01.100
and he's already awake again.

00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:06.100
But the news is Lewis has over 10 ulcers

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:09.533
in his small intestine.

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:13.393
Good gracious, this does not sound good.

00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:17.020
- Not the stomach, the small intestine.

00:13:17.020 --> 00:13:19.420
- Yeah, the duodenum.

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:20.782
Duo denim.

00:13:20.782 --> 00:13:22.544
I don't know how you say it.

00:13:22.544 --> 00:13:23.377
- You know if you take that out,

00:13:23.377 --> 00:13:25.260
you can stretch it around
the earth nine times.

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:26.430
(Link chuckles)

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:28.140
- Just Lewis's, you know how long his is?

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:30.310
- Yeah yeah, just an individual's.

00:13:30.310 --> 00:13:31.443
- Oh okay.
- Mm-hmm.

00:13:31.443 --> 00:13:33.530
- That can't be true.

00:13:33.530 --> 00:13:35.060
But it--
- That's how quickly

00:13:35.060 --> 00:13:38.020
light travels, the speed of
light I think around the earth.

00:13:38.020 --> 00:13:40.580
Nine times in a second, I don't know.

00:13:40.580 --> 00:13:42.510
I'm confusing facts.

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:45.773
- The doctor gave us the
good news that even though,

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:49.000
I don't know if this is a southern saying,

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:50.770
'cause the doctor didn't say it.

00:13:50.770 --> 00:13:54.420
He was from LA, he wouldn't,
but what I would say is,

00:13:54.420 --> 00:13:57.183
he's eat up with ulcers,
he's eat up with them.

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:00.820
But he said there was one in
particular that looked bigger

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:03.610
and it had a scab that had formed over it

00:14:03.610 --> 00:14:05.490
just like skin on the
outside of your body.

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:06.323
- This is tough to listen to.

00:14:06.323 --> 00:14:08.170
- I'm sorry.

00:14:08.170 --> 00:14:10.120
I'm surprised I'm able to talk about it.

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:13.850
We can get into that more later
but he said that just like

00:14:13.850 --> 00:14:16.120
skin on the outside of
your body on the inside

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:21.120
of your intestine, it forms a
scab with which then it heals.

00:14:22.580 --> 00:14:26.060
And he said I agitated the scab.

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:27.990
It's amazing, you put the scope down there

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:31.470
with a camera and take
pictures, you see the live video

00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:35.381
and you can blast water
and also medication

00:14:35.381 --> 00:14:37.417
through this scope.

00:14:37.417 --> 00:14:41.148
- And they can control it like a

00:14:41.148 --> 00:14:42.710
(mimics zapping sound) kinda thing,

00:14:42.710 --> 00:14:43.920
like once it gets down there?

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:45.798
- It was actually very easy to control.

00:14:45.798 --> 00:14:46.791
- You can manipulate it.

00:14:46.791 --> 00:14:48.430
- He said, "You wanna try it?"

00:14:48.430 --> 00:14:50.670
And then it was like two joysticks.

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:53.160
No I didn't see the thing, man.

00:14:53.160 --> 00:14:56.070
We went out, I don't know how
many joysticks there were.

00:14:56.070 --> 00:14:58.490
- I'm just assuming
that to be that precise,

00:14:58.490 --> 00:15:02.870
it has some sort of mechanical head on it.

00:15:02.870 --> 00:15:06.520
- At the moment I was not asking
about the mechanical head.

00:15:06.520 --> 00:15:08.350
- Again, that's what I'd
do in these situations

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:10.020
'cause I don't want to
talk about the real--

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:10.853
- You deflect.

00:15:11.790 --> 00:15:13.370
- Let's talk about the
mechanics of the machinery

00:15:13.370 --> 00:15:14.466
that's inside of him right now.

00:15:14.466 --> 00:15:15.877
(Link chuckles)

00:15:15.877 --> 00:15:17.040
How 'bout that?

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:18.510
- But he said he sprayed
it with a little water

00:15:18.510 --> 00:15:20.440
and then all evidence pointed to the fact

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:23.260
that it was gonna stay which is great news

00:15:23.260 --> 00:15:26.840
and it also meant that
if they monitored him

00:15:26.840 --> 00:15:28.710
and there was no more bleeding,

00:15:28.710 --> 00:15:31.250
that that meant that there
wasn't any other source

00:15:31.250 --> 00:15:35.470
of bleeding lower down in
the rest of his GI tract.

00:15:35.470 --> 00:15:38.420
'Cause to do a colonoscopy,
to go from the top up,

00:15:38.420 --> 00:15:40.640
you have to--
- The bottom of him.

00:15:40.640 --> 00:15:43.170
- Yeah from the bottom
up, you'd have to wait

00:15:43.170 --> 00:15:46.830
to totally clean out the entire system.

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:49.940
And that was just more time, but anyway,

00:15:49.940 --> 00:15:51.620
at that point we were in a holding pattern

00:15:51.620 --> 00:15:52.453
and because it's the ICU--

00:15:52.453 --> 00:15:53.770
- Because that equipment
is very expensive,

00:15:53.770 --> 00:15:55.374
you don't want, all over it.

00:15:55.374 --> 00:15:57.350
- (chuckles) Yes, Rhett.

00:15:57.350 --> 00:15:58.993
That's exactly what it is.

00:16:01.020 --> 00:16:03.183
I'm trying not to be that graphic.

00:16:04.030 --> 00:16:06.630
In the ICU, they make everyone leave

00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:08.330
while they have shift change.

00:16:08.330 --> 00:16:12.890
So just so happens that
we had made reservations

00:16:12.890 --> 00:16:14.960
for this Japanese steakhouse
before we knew any

00:16:14.960 --> 00:16:16.050
of this was gonna happen.
- Yeah take a break,

00:16:16.050 --> 00:16:16.883
go eat some Japanese.

00:16:16.883 --> 00:16:19.650
- So we're gonna, you make
me feel horrible about it

00:16:19.650 --> 00:16:22.490
but Christy was like
I'm gonna stay around.

00:16:22.490 --> 00:16:26.110
- We have reservations, Link. (chuckles)

00:16:26.110 --> 00:16:29.670
- Granny, why don't
you go and see the kids

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:31.320
and just take a break
because we're not allowed

00:16:31.320 --> 00:16:33.347
to be in here anyway.
- Yeah.

00:16:33.347 --> 00:16:34.600
- And it's very close.

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:36.450
- And was it one where they
throw the shrimp in the hat?

00:16:36.450 --> 00:16:37.660
- Yeah they throw the shrimp--

00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:39.210
- And the fire volcano made of onions.

00:16:39.210 --> 00:16:40.360
- I thought that would be nice.

00:16:40.360 --> 00:16:41.510
- Oh yeah it's always nice.

00:16:41.510 --> 00:16:44.900
- So Christy stayed back,
so she was very much

00:16:44.900 --> 00:16:47.960
in proximity even though she
wasn't allowed to be in there.

00:16:47.960 --> 00:16:50.720
And then I took Mom,
because we had good news

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:52.350
and we were in a holding pattern--

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:53.360
- Oh so you went to the
Japanese steakhouse.

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:57.130
- I went too, yeah me and
Mom met Britton and the kids

00:16:57.130 --> 00:17:00.250
that we met at the Japanese steakhouse.

00:17:00.250 --> 00:17:02.900
- Did you get a combo of
chicken, shrimp and beef?

00:17:02.900 --> 00:17:03.833
- Yes.

00:17:05.020 --> 00:17:07.120
You're deflecting now.

00:17:07.120 --> 00:17:10.695
- No, I really am interested
in what people order at that.

00:17:10.695 --> 00:17:12.050
I always get the combo.

00:17:12.050 --> 00:17:15.700
- And we're all on edge
but it was nice for her

00:17:15.700 --> 00:17:18.090
to see the kids 'cause otherwise,

00:17:18.090 --> 00:17:21.239
she wasn't able to see them
and I go up to use the restroom

00:17:21.239 --> 00:17:23.420
right before he starts flippin' shrimp

00:17:23.420 --> 00:17:24.591
and as I'm walking into
the restroom, I look up--

00:17:24.591 --> 00:17:25.530
- Shrimp.

00:17:25.530 --> 00:17:29.590
- And daggum, if it
isn't the scope doctor.

00:17:29.590 --> 00:17:31.580
The frickin' scope doctor,

00:17:31.580 --> 00:17:33.330
the gastro guy--
- Oh okay.

00:17:33.330 --> 00:17:34.403
- Was at the Japanese restaurant.

00:17:34.403 --> 00:17:35.840
- I heard you guys talking about this

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:38.000
but I didn't understand
that this was the story.

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:40.570
- Yeah he was one of those
hibachi guys, he worked there.

00:17:40.570 --> 00:17:41.540
- Oh yeah well you know what,

00:17:41.540 --> 00:17:42.940
doctors these days
don't make enough money.

00:17:42.940 --> 00:17:44.047
- Right.
- You gotta have

00:17:44.047 --> 00:17:45.370
a backup plan.
- He was there

00:17:45.370 --> 00:17:46.900
with his family at another table

00:17:46.900 --> 00:17:51.440
and I went up, I was like,
I found myself upon him

00:17:51.440 --> 00:17:54.990
by surprise on my way to the
bathroom so I'm in his face

00:17:54.990 --> 00:17:57.437
and I'm like I recognized
him and I'm like,

00:17:57.437 --> 00:17:59.940
"Hey doc, you just...

00:18:01.690 --> 00:18:03.640
"You were just treating my father-in-law."

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:04.473
- Right, yeah.

00:18:04.473 --> 00:18:07.580
- And again, I just came
out with father-in-law.

00:18:07.580 --> 00:18:08.690
And it worked.

00:18:08.690 --> 00:18:09.890
- Yeah 'cause he doesn't know.

00:18:09.890 --> 00:18:12.001
- And he understands that it's not,

00:18:12.001 --> 00:18:13.800
I don't know what he
understood but it was easy.

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:14.919
- Right he just got the chicken though

00:18:14.919 --> 00:18:16.170
because he's a doctor.

00:18:16.170 --> 00:18:18.240
- It was weird 'cause it was
like one of those moments

00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:22.210
where remember in, well I
remember in middle school

00:18:22.210 --> 00:18:24.850
when I went to Walmart one time when I was

00:18:24.850 --> 00:18:29.180
in third grade and I saw Miss
Hobbs, my third grade teacher

00:18:29.180 --> 00:18:32.090
in the chips aisle at Walmart.
- And you're like what,

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:34.780
they can be outside of school as well?

00:18:34.780 --> 00:18:37.860
- And she started talking to
me and I felt really weird

00:18:37.860 --> 00:18:39.753
and I got embarrassed and I was like,

00:18:39.753 --> 00:18:42.320
(babbling) I don't know how to talk.

00:18:42.320 --> 00:18:44.610
- Did I get grade on this conversation?

00:18:44.610 --> 00:18:49.610
- Yeah I felt like a third-grader
in the hibachi restaurant

00:18:49.610 --> 00:18:51.380
talking to my teacher.
- Right.

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:52.580
- It was weird.

00:18:52.580 --> 00:18:55.260
But he was very nice and he
came over and he talked to Mom

00:18:55.260 --> 00:18:57.430
and she was very appreciative
because we had good news

00:18:57.430 --> 00:18:58.263
at that point.
- And he's like

00:18:58.263 --> 00:18:59.730
you wanna see some pictures of the ulcers?

00:18:59.730 --> 00:19:01.191
- He had already shown us those.

00:19:01.191 --> 00:19:03.823
- But not at the restaurant.
- Yeah but not right there.

00:19:05.460 --> 00:19:07.901
And then I was like, we
brought the kids back

00:19:07.901 --> 00:19:11.410
and we took them up to see
Lewis when they allowed us

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:15.380
to go back in the ICU and
the nurse had a funny look

00:19:15.380 --> 00:19:19.130
on her face and I looked
around into the room

00:19:19.130 --> 00:19:22.240
and Lewis did not look good
at all, he looked worse,

00:19:22.240 --> 00:19:25.823
and then the kids said hello
and then it was kinda like,

00:19:26.850 --> 00:19:28.750
Christy why don't you just
take the kids back home.

00:19:28.750 --> 00:19:30.900
I'll stay here with Mom and
then I'll Uber back later

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:32.190
'cause the plan was we were all leaving

00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:35.240
and Mom was just gonna stay but...

00:19:37.640 --> 00:19:40.110
It took awhile for it to really sink in,

00:19:40.110 --> 00:19:45.110
but what had happened was he
had started passing blood--

00:19:46.900 --> 00:19:48.690
- Again.
- Again.

00:19:48.690 --> 00:19:51.236
- And this sounds like a
great place for a teaser.

00:19:51.236 --> 00:19:52.333
(chuckles)
- Oh gosh.

00:19:53.230 --> 00:19:56.110
- Because we need to do a short break

00:19:56.110 --> 00:19:59.330
and let people know, oh things got worse.

00:19:59.330 --> 00:20:02.030
- Things get worse before
they get better but they do.

00:20:03.550 --> 00:20:04.837
- But we are gonna take a short break

00:20:04.837 --> 00:20:07.970
and let you know that
you can get these mugs

00:20:07.970 --> 00:20:09.070
we're drinking out of.

00:20:10.260 --> 00:20:11.770
You can't get this one.

00:20:11.770 --> 00:20:14.730
This was a prototype, I don't
even know why I've got it.

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:17.030
This is just to tempt you
with something you can't buy.

00:20:17.030 --> 00:20:19.970
- We prototype all types
of things in order to land

00:20:19.970 --> 00:20:23.573
on the perfect thing for
your mouths, your bodies,

00:20:24.560 --> 00:20:25.530
your hairs.

00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:27.660
- I'm trying to blend in completely today.

00:20:27.660 --> 00:20:29.870
As you can see, I have
two different brown mugs,

00:20:29.870 --> 00:20:34.570
I have a brown hoodie
on, I have brownish hair.

00:20:34.570 --> 00:20:36.370
- Brownish skin.
- It's all kind of blending

00:20:36.370 --> 00:20:38.870
in with the background and I kinda think

00:20:38.870 --> 00:20:39.703
this might be my new thing.

00:20:39.703 --> 00:20:41.800
- What are you selling?

00:20:41.800 --> 00:20:43.290
You're having focus issues today.

00:20:43.290 --> 00:20:45.120
- I'm selling beigeness.

00:20:45.120 --> 00:20:46.970
- That's not for sale.

00:20:46.970 --> 00:20:49.420
- It kinda is though, you'll see,

00:20:49.420 --> 00:20:52.380
you'll understand what I'm
gettin' at months from now

00:20:52.380 --> 00:20:55.330
but in the meantime what
you can buy is these mugs

00:20:55.330 --> 00:20:57.180
and lots of other cool things over at--

00:20:57.180 --> 00:20:59.913
- Mythical.store--
- Dot store.

00:21:03.330 --> 00:21:06.120
- The hindsight of the experience,

00:21:06.120 --> 00:21:08.120
it just seems much more clear than when,

00:21:09.004 --> 00:21:11.943
when things are progressing
in the hospital,

00:21:14.690 --> 00:21:17.090
the nurses and doctors
are reacting to things

00:21:17.090 --> 00:21:19.810
and it's not that
they're not communicating

00:21:19.810 --> 00:21:22.410
but it's the combination
of them doing their jobs,

00:21:22.410 --> 00:21:24.660
trying to communicate but
also the ability for that

00:21:24.660 --> 00:21:28.133
to sink in, to be able to
receive the information.

00:21:28.133 --> 00:21:30.776
The doctor would just drop in occasionally

00:21:30.776 --> 00:21:34.230
and it's go time to have your questions.

00:21:34.230 --> 00:21:36.730
- Could you imagine just--
- And that's difficult.

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:39.223
- Could you imagine being the person?

00:21:40.780 --> 00:21:42.660
Neither one of us would be good at that.

00:21:42.660 --> 00:21:44.510
We might be good at--
- You mean the patient?

00:21:44.510 --> 00:21:46.850
- No the nurse.
- Oh gosh.

00:21:46.850 --> 00:21:50.030
- Having to do something
but then also explain

00:21:50.030 --> 00:21:54.470
to someone in a sympathetic
tone what's actually happening.

00:21:54.470 --> 00:21:55.303
Forget about it.

00:21:55.303 --> 00:21:57.840
- One of my big takeaways
from this thing is

00:21:57.840 --> 00:22:02.840
I have just a blossomed appreciation,

00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:09.323
specifically for nurses
in a hospital environment.

00:22:10.157 --> 00:22:15.157
They are amazing people who
do an amazingly difficult job

00:22:16.010 --> 00:22:18.320
and that's a big part of it,

00:22:18.320 --> 00:22:20.220
interacting not only with the patient but

00:22:20.220 --> 00:22:23.543
with the loved ones who are there present.

00:22:24.600 --> 00:22:28.560
And for me I'm bringing my kids,
one of my kids is too young

00:22:28.560 --> 00:22:30.250
to actually be there and
I'm breaking the rules

00:22:30.250 --> 00:22:32.258
for him to come up so--

00:22:32.258 --> 00:22:34.100
- Really, what's the age limit?

00:22:34.100 --> 00:22:36.280
- 14, in the ICU.

00:22:36.280 --> 00:22:37.960
- And they let you do that?

00:22:37.960 --> 00:22:40.530
- Well yeah, a 14 year old
but not a nine year old.

00:22:40.530 --> 00:22:42.150
- No but you were able to get Lando in.

00:22:42.150 --> 00:22:44.750
- I just kinda went up with him.

00:22:44.750 --> 00:22:46.850
- Oh.
- 'Cause I was like,

00:22:46.850 --> 00:22:49.970
Lewis is fine and his
spirits will be lifted

00:22:49.970 --> 00:22:52.350
by seeing you for a minute.

00:22:52.350 --> 00:22:55.160
That was not smart, I should
have respect that rule.

00:22:55.160 --> 00:22:57.610
The nurse actually told
me he could see things

00:22:57.610 --> 00:23:00.590
in the ICU that--
- He will never forget.

00:23:00.590 --> 00:23:01.953
- A nine year old may not want to see.

00:23:01.953 --> 00:23:03.918
- Will be etched on his brain forever.

00:23:03.918 --> 00:23:06.470
- I was like wow, I'm an idiot

00:23:06.470 --> 00:23:08.090
and you really made a good point.

00:23:08.090 --> 00:23:10.040
I'm gonna take my kids out of here now.

00:23:11.175 --> 00:23:14.510
My head space was so,

00:23:14.510 --> 00:23:16.500
this is gonna be over at any moment.

00:23:16.500 --> 00:23:19.040
This is gonna be okay,
we already got good news.

00:23:19.040 --> 00:23:22.120
Yes he has ulcers, yes he's
gotta figure that stuff out

00:23:22.120 --> 00:23:24.390
and there's gonna be
more treatment to come

00:23:24.390 --> 00:23:25.663
when he gets back home.

00:23:27.180 --> 00:23:29.850
But he's in the clear.

00:23:29.850 --> 00:23:32.696
When we got back, Christy
took the kids away

00:23:32.696 --> 00:23:35.440
and they had already been giving him blood

00:23:35.440 --> 00:23:37.720
because he had lost blood--
- Already.

00:23:37.720 --> 00:23:38.783
- The night before.

00:23:40.270 --> 00:23:44.290
I think by this point,
they were giving him six,

00:23:44.290 --> 00:23:47.440
seven, eight pints of blood.

00:23:47.440 --> 00:23:49.080
- Whoa, whoa, whoa, already.

00:23:49.080 --> 00:23:49.913
- I'm telling you.

00:23:49.913 --> 00:23:53.230
I was there, this was 10 o'clock at night,

00:23:53.230 --> 00:23:56.020
11 o'clock at night, midnight, and then--

00:23:56.020 --> 00:23:58.370
- 'Cause you don't have that much,

00:23:58.370 --> 00:24:00.310
I told you about this recently.

00:24:00.310 --> 00:24:03.810
- Yeah for the book we had to,
oh you don't wanna say that?

00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:06.130
- Well no, okay...

00:24:07.330 --> 00:24:10.940
I had to look up for a part
of the book we're working on

00:24:12.930 --> 00:24:14.690
how much blood is in a human body.

00:24:14.690 --> 00:24:16.700
- Yeah, and what's the answer?

00:24:16.700 --> 00:24:18.210
- I think the average adult just has

00:24:18.210 --> 00:24:20.200
like two gallons of blood.

00:24:20.200 --> 00:24:22.390
- Well how many pints is that?

00:24:22.390 --> 00:24:23.223
- I don't know.

00:24:23.223 --> 00:24:24.453
- Three pints in a gallon?

00:24:25.890 --> 00:24:26.980
- Hopefully more than that.

00:24:26.980 --> 00:24:30.050
- Four, I thought there was
only four pints in a gallon.

00:24:30.050 --> 00:24:32.090
- All I'm saying is--

00:24:32.090 --> 00:24:34.000
- Kiko will look it up 'cause we're stupe.

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:36.750
- There's a lot less blood in a human body

00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:39.500
and so giving him that much blood,

00:24:39.500 --> 00:24:40.900
like he didn't have hardly any.

00:24:40.900 --> 00:24:45.900
- Well and then the thing is,

00:24:45.970 --> 00:24:49.240
he was confined to the bed--

00:24:49.240 --> 00:24:50.190
- What is it, Kiko?

00:24:51.643 --> 00:24:52.820
- Eight pints to a gallon.
- Eight pints

00:24:52.820 --> 00:24:54.630
and then how many, you said
there were how many gallons

00:24:54.630 --> 00:24:56.250
in a body?
- Like two gallons of blood

00:24:56.250 --> 00:24:57.820
in a body.

00:24:57.820 --> 00:25:00.290
- So 16 pints.

00:25:00.290 --> 00:25:02.410
- Look that up too, I wanna make sure.

00:25:02.410 --> 00:25:04.993
- How many pints of
blood in a average body.

00:25:08.310 --> 00:25:10.050
'Cause I will say by the end of this,

00:25:10.050 --> 00:25:13.073
they gave him 12 pints of blood.

00:25:14.180 --> 00:25:15.720
How many?

00:25:15.720 --> 00:25:17.460
- 12 pints, so that's, wow,

00:25:17.460 --> 00:25:20.410
so about a gallon and a half
of blood in an average person.

00:25:20.410 --> 00:25:22.300
- But I'm telling you right now,

00:25:22.300 --> 00:25:27.240
they gave him enough blood
that fills up a human body.

00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:30.100
- That's crazy, that's what
so surprised me 'cause--

00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:32.850
- Yes and the more it, and again,

00:25:32.850 --> 00:25:35.030
they kept giving him, when
they were giving him two,

00:25:35.030 --> 00:25:37.190
three, four, five,

00:25:37.190 --> 00:25:40.270
concerns start to raise, my man,

00:25:40.270 --> 00:25:43.090
and they said okay,

00:25:43.090 --> 00:25:48.090
we're gonna to have to
give him a central line.

00:25:48.640 --> 00:25:53.640
We can't use the IVs to keep
up with him passing the blood.

00:25:53.750 --> 00:25:56.400
And it was a very visceral experience

00:25:56.400 --> 00:25:58.700
which he doesn't remember
any of even though

00:25:58.700 --> 00:26:01.700
he was conscious, he was talking,

00:26:01.700 --> 00:26:05.461
but he wasn't very lucid.

00:26:05.461 --> 00:26:07.264
- Yeah.

00:26:07.264 --> 00:26:09.880
- As you can imagine.
- Right.

00:26:09.880 --> 00:26:11.710
- And in that situation,
you can't move him out

00:26:11.710 --> 00:26:14.403
of the bed and he's passing blood.

00:26:15.739 --> 00:26:20.400
It's a visceral and horrible situation

00:26:20.400 --> 00:26:21.970
to be bedside for that.

00:26:21.970 --> 00:26:24.833
- Okay this is coming from the guy who,

00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:29.387
you can't take any amount of blood

00:26:29.387 --> 00:26:31.870
and the thought of needles,

00:26:31.870 --> 00:26:34.668
and they're sitting there
filling your father-in-law.

00:26:34.668 --> 00:26:36.230
(chuckles)
- Yeah just fillin' him up.

00:26:36.230 --> 00:26:38.040
- With, I mean how--
- He's like a fountain,

00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:39.110
it's just passing through him.

00:26:39.110 --> 00:26:42.163
- Did you go into a mental space,

00:26:43.210 --> 00:26:47.403
like a un-before-tapped,

00:26:48.540 --> 00:26:50.470
never before tapped mental space in order

00:26:50.470 --> 00:26:52.320
to get through this without fainting?

00:26:53.625 --> 00:26:55.907
- Earlier on whenever
he started passing blood

00:26:55.907 --> 00:26:58.593
and they would clean him
up, I would step out.

00:27:00.810 --> 00:27:04.374
But I felt like that was
just to give him privacy

00:27:04.374 --> 00:27:06.343
and for them to do their job.

00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:08.860
- So you were--
- And then I would

00:27:08.860 --> 00:27:10.680
come back in, and I was in a zone.

00:27:10.680 --> 00:27:13.680
- You were in a mode, a zone.
- I guess I was in a zone of,

00:27:15.030 --> 00:27:16.080
it was kind of a...

00:27:18.768 --> 00:27:20.190
It was a bit of a fog.

00:27:20.190 --> 00:27:23.410
It was not realizing how
dire things were getting

00:27:23.410 --> 00:27:25.700
but then when they said we
need to give him a central line

00:27:25.700 --> 00:27:29.390
in his neck so that we can pump blood in

00:27:29.390 --> 00:27:32.720
and keep up with how
quickly he's losing blood

00:27:32.720 --> 00:27:35.160
and we're getting the doctor back in here

00:27:35.160 --> 00:27:37.540
to do another scope in order to see

00:27:37.540 --> 00:27:40.070
where this new bleeding is coming from.

00:27:40.070 --> 00:27:41.635
- Well just goes to show you not only

00:27:41.635 --> 00:27:44.271
how important blood is.
- Yeah.

00:27:44.271 --> 00:27:47.210
- There are these places, there
are so many places on earth

00:27:47.210 --> 00:27:52.210
that don't even have adequate
amounts of clean blood.

00:27:52.410 --> 00:27:55.070
- Yeah, and yeah.

00:27:55.070 --> 00:27:56.900
- Privilege just to be in
a place that you can lose

00:27:56.900 --> 00:27:59.500
that much blood and immediately
have it replenished.

00:28:00.590 --> 00:28:03.980
I don't know what the PSA
is here but give blood.

00:28:03.980 --> 00:28:05.763
- I thought about that too.

00:28:07.597 --> 00:28:10.703
- And also be willing to take blood.

00:28:12.252 --> 00:28:13.085
I don't know there's some people--

00:28:13.085 --> 00:28:14.630
- There was never a
conversation about that.

00:28:14.630 --> 00:28:16.215
- Well I know but--

00:28:16.215 --> 00:28:19.060
- There are people who--
- If you currently subscribe

00:28:19.060 --> 00:28:23.270
to a worldview that doesn't
allow you to take blood,

00:28:23.270 --> 00:28:24.980
reevaluate that world view.

00:28:24.980 --> 00:28:27.410
- Well he would have died.
- That's my second PSA.

00:28:27.410 --> 00:28:28.660
- He would have died
within a few hours we get

00:28:28.660 --> 00:28:30.976
to the hospital, he wouldn't
have anywhere close to this

00:28:30.976 --> 00:28:32.703
if he couldn't take blood.

00:28:34.804 --> 00:28:38.640
They couldn't find a central
line in his neck that worked

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:40.750
and they had to do a
central line in his groin

00:28:40.750 --> 00:28:44.070
and then basically his hip.

00:28:44.070 --> 00:28:45.460
And then they're pumping him full of blood

00:28:45.460 --> 00:28:48.060
in order to just gain time
and it was at that point

00:28:48.060 --> 00:28:50.630
that I realized that

00:28:52.030 --> 00:28:53.730
oh my gosh, this is--

00:28:53.730 --> 00:28:56.226
- Serious.
- This is very dire.

00:28:56.226 --> 00:28:58.300
This is touch and go.

00:28:58.300 --> 00:29:00.310
- How's your mom handling
this at the time?

00:29:00.310 --> 00:29:03.680
- She was in more, I wouldn't
call what she was in a fog,

00:29:03.680 --> 00:29:05.810
she was in more of a supportive,

00:29:05.810 --> 00:29:09.468
she was very positive.

00:29:09.468 --> 00:29:14.468
And she was going to work in
terms of just being positive

00:29:14.700 --> 00:29:16.490
and in support mode, it was--

00:29:16.490 --> 00:29:17.530
- And she come from--
- It was amazing.

00:29:17.530 --> 00:29:19.630
- She's come from a
medical background being

00:29:19.630 --> 00:29:21.320
in like a clinical place.

00:29:21.320 --> 00:29:23.195
- Yeah, she took blood for a living

00:29:23.195 --> 00:29:25.810
and she's used to being around blood

00:29:25.810 --> 00:29:29.110
and medical environs.
- Yeah.

00:29:29.110 --> 00:29:32.212
- So she wasn't shaken up
by that but she said later

00:29:32.212 --> 00:29:34.560
that it dawned on her the next morning

00:29:34.560 --> 00:29:35.890
how dire things were there.

00:29:35.890 --> 00:29:37.490
I just think that she was in a mode

00:29:37.490 --> 00:29:42.490
where she wasn't allowing
herself to go to the dark

00:29:45.260 --> 00:29:47.110
potentials, okay.

00:29:47.110 --> 00:29:49.300
- The dark potentials.
- The dark potentials.

00:29:49.300 --> 00:29:51.060
- That's the name of our second novel.

00:29:51.060 --> 00:29:55.819
- But the doctor finally showed
up and they got ahead of it,

00:29:55.819 --> 00:29:57.910
the bleeding a little bit but

00:30:00.770 --> 00:30:05.110
it was scary because there
was blood all over the bed.

00:30:05.110 --> 00:30:05.943
- Mm.

00:30:05.943 --> 00:30:08.160
- And they couldn't keep up with it

00:30:08.160 --> 00:30:12.930
so they would put a blanket
down over him and...

00:30:16.630 --> 00:30:19.936
He was disoriented so at
times he would look down

00:30:19.936 --> 00:30:24.110
and he would move the blanket
and he would see all the blood

00:30:24.110 --> 00:30:28.350
or he wouldn't see it and
his hand would go down there

00:30:28.350 --> 00:30:29.543
to feel what's going on.

00:30:29.543 --> 00:30:31.740
- This is tough for me
to listen to, I'm sorry.

00:30:31.740 --> 00:30:34.360
- I guess the reason--
(Rhett sighs)

00:30:34.360 --> 00:30:37.310
The reason why I share it is because

00:30:41.020 --> 00:30:42.090
I think...

00:30:45.710 --> 00:30:48.040
I could never imagine
that I would be bedside

00:30:48.040 --> 00:30:51.650
for a situation like that with my mom

00:30:51.650 --> 00:30:55.130
and I'm glad that we were
able to be supportive

00:30:55.130 --> 00:30:58.840
but it was very scary and
I never would have thought

00:30:58.840 --> 00:31:01.460
that I would have been able to be,

00:31:01.460 --> 00:31:03.850
to contribute to a supportive environment

00:31:03.850 --> 00:31:05.483
when it was that scary.

00:31:06.440 --> 00:31:07.540
- Well just, okay--

00:31:07.540 --> 00:31:11.580
- So I'm surprised, I'm not bragging.

00:31:11.580 --> 00:31:14.155
I'm just saying that these things happen

00:31:14.155 --> 00:31:18.250
and maybe you go in a mode

00:31:18.250 --> 00:31:19.173
but if you don't,

00:31:20.500 --> 00:31:24.060
I feel more prepared
having gone through it

00:31:24.060 --> 00:31:26.460
to be more supportive--
- Well I do think that,

00:31:27.320 --> 00:31:32.010
I will say that the human
brain is really complex

00:31:32.010 --> 00:31:34.770
and I think that what ends up
happening a lot of times is

00:31:34.770 --> 00:31:37.420
you know that you can't
afford to check out.

00:31:37.420 --> 00:31:39.760
I think there are certain
situations where you can afford

00:31:39.760 --> 00:31:41.700
to check out, like when you
cut yourself at Christmas,

00:31:41.700 --> 00:31:43.160
it's like it's not that big of a deal.

00:31:43.160 --> 00:31:45.470
- And faint into your
real father-in-law's arms,

00:31:45.470 --> 00:31:46.303
which I did.

00:31:49.970 --> 00:31:51.093
- But my question is,

00:31:53.750 --> 00:31:55.920
why were you the one that was there?

00:31:55.920 --> 00:32:00.460
It was just circumstances
because of the way

00:32:00.460 --> 00:32:02.110
that you brought the kids in and then,

00:32:02.110 --> 00:32:03.520
you didn't know how bad it was gonna get.

00:32:03.520 --> 00:32:06.570
'Cause if you think about
how bad it was going to get

00:32:06.570 --> 00:32:09.540
and Christy and you both knew
how bad it was going to get

00:32:09.540 --> 00:32:12.110
and you had to make a
decision who was gonna stay,

00:32:12.110 --> 00:32:13.920
you probably would have
picked Christy to stay

00:32:13.920 --> 00:32:15.500
and you would have gone
home with the kids.

00:32:15.500 --> 00:32:17.870
Or was it because it was your relations?

00:32:17.870 --> 00:32:21.730
- Yeah it was because, Mom, well...

00:32:24.740 --> 00:32:28.190
I'll back into that 'cause
I do think it's interesting.

00:32:28.190 --> 00:32:32.580
I feel so much and I felt
so much for Lewis and Mom

00:32:32.580 --> 00:32:35.615
because they don't travel
and then they find themselves

00:32:35.615 --> 00:32:39.690
going through this
situation so far from home

00:32:39.690 --> 00:32:44.253
with no support structure
beyond me and Christy.

00:32:44.253 --> 00:32:46.240
- Right.
- They know no one else

00:32:46.240 --> 00:32:49.890
except they might have met
people in passing through us

00:32:49.890 --> 00:32:52.550
but there's no one, like back home,

00:32:52.550 --> 00:32:57.283
Lewis has an extended family
that they're very tight-knit.

00:33:00.954 --> 00:33:04.630
So his real name is Edd, I
think Lewis is his middle name.

00:33:04.630 --> 00:33:06.610
Edd with two Ds, and then the hospital,

00:33:06.610 --> 00:33:09.068
they kept asking him, "What's your name?"

00:33:09.068 --> 00:33:11.685
And then in order to give
medication or do treatment

00:33:11.685 --> 00:33:14.580
or just know if he was lucid,
they would ask him his name

00:33:14.580 --> 00:33:16.190
and they were expecting him to say Edd

00:33:16.190 --> 00:33:18.200
and he would say Lewis so they would think

00:33:18.200 --> 00:33:22.220
it was worse than it was
but his brother, Edd.

00:33:22.220 --> 00:33:24.053
He's got a brother Ned, Ted, and Fred.

00:33:24.053 --> 00:33:25.978
- Yeah it's unbelievable. (chuckles)

00:33:25.978 --> 00:33:28.714
- And you better believe they
all would have been there

00:33:28.714 --> 00:33:33.070
as well as his children and
everybody else who loves him

00:33:33.070 --> 00:33:35.350
and obviously would have
been there to support him.

00:33:35.350 --> 00:33:36.983
- Ned, Ted, Fred and Edd.

00:33:38.560 --> 00:33:39.650
- And Peggy.

00:33:39.650 --> 00:33:44.510
- Yeah there's not a
good typical woman name.

00:33:44.510 --> 00:33:45.825
- They call her Ped.

00:33:45.825 --> 00:33:47.373
(Rhett chuckles)
No they don't.

00:33:48.290 --> 00:33:51.173
There may be other siblings,
I can't remember, but anyway,

00:33:52.360 --> 00:33:55.700
because they're out here,
it's just me and Christy

00:33:55.700 --> 00:34:00.700
with Mom to, we find ourselves
in this weird situation

00:34:00.740 --> 00:34:04.560
where we're the only
support that they've got

00:34:04.560 --> 00:34:08.410
so when we had to send the
kids, I was like, well,

00:34:08.410 --> 00:34:10.020
Mom's gonna stay there for Lewis

00:34:10.020 --> 00:34:12.350
and I'm gonna stay there for Mom.

00:34:12.350 --> 00:34:14.530
I'm gonna support Mom and
Mom's gonna support Lewis

00:34:14.530 --> 00:34:17.160
and then honestly that's the first way

00:34:17.160 --> 00:34:18.380
that I thought about it.

00:34:18.380 --> 00:34:22.620
I think that changed over that
night and the next few days

00:34:22.620 --> 00:34:25.770
but we can talk about that some more

00:34:25.770 --> 00:34:30.034
but at this point, yeah, we
did actively make that decision

00:34:30.034 --> 00:34:34.210
for me to be there with
mom and so by 1 a.m.,

00:34:34.210 --> 00:34:36.630
the doctor's showing back
up and doing the scope

00:34:36.630 --> 00:34:40.977
and finding that that
problem ulcer was basically

00:34:40.977 --> 00:34:43.535
at this point, obviously gushing blood.

00:34:43.535 --> 00:34:45.440
- Ugh (winces).

00:34:45.440 --> 00:34:46.686
- And then to use that same scope--

00:34:46.686 --> 00:34:48.317
- You gotta put a trigger
warning on this episode.

00:34:48.317 --> 00:34:50.567
(chuckles)

00:34:51.770 --> 00:34:54.140
- I think maybe I'm able
to talk about this in a way

00:34:54.140 --> 00:34:56.010
that maybe I feel
differently about blood now.

00:34:56.010 --> 00:34:57.670
- Well but you're also the one saying it

00:34:57.670 --> 00:35:00.593
so it's not happening unexpected.

00:35:02.642 --> 00:35:04.480
- He's able to use that scope

00:35:06.020 --> 00:35:07.860
in his hibachi hands.

00:35:07.860 --> 00:35:10.170
- Right, he's still got
chicken grease on him.

00:35:10.170 --> 00:35:13.430
- And he sprayed some epinephrin.

00:35:13.430 --> 00:35:16.380
- You think he put the
redneck sauce on his chicken?

00:35:16.380 --> 00:35:18.110
- He puts redneck sauce on that ulcer

00:35:18.110 --> 00:35:19.580
and it just sutures right up.

00:35:19.580 --> 00:35:21.840
- The creamy sauce that you
can get at the hibachi place

00:35:21.840 --> 00:35:23.250
we always call--
- Redneck sauce.

00:35:23.250 --> 00:35:27.100
- Because only, you go over to Japan,

00:35:27.100 --> 00:35:29.320
they don't have that sauce, okay.

00:35:29.320 --> 00:35:31.610
That's something rednecks came up with.

00:35:31.610 --> 00:35:33.440
- It's good.
- Now it's good. (chuckles)

00:35:33.440 --> 00:35:34.684
- I took the whole bowl that night

00:35:34.684 --> 00:35:36.730
and I just poured it all over my plate.

00:35:36.730 --> 00:35:37.610
I was like--

00:35:37.610 --> 00:35:39.380
- I'm a redneck.
- Yeah.

00:35:39.380 --> 00:35:40.761
I could feel my mullet growing.

00:35:40.761 --> 00:35:42.240
(Rhett chuckles)

00:35:42.240 --> 00:35:44.474
So he--
- Epinephrin.

00:35:44.474 --> 00:35:45.626
- He put some epinephrin on it

00:35:45.626 --> 00:35:47.397
and it's basically sutured it up.

00:35:47.397 --> 00:35:48.960
- It's a vasoconstrictor.

00:35:48.960 --> 00:35:50.930
- And then--
- Who knew?

00:35:50.930 --> 00:35:52.520
- And he showed us, well he did.

00:35:52.520 --> 00:35:54.340
And he showed us pictures of it.

00:35:54.340 --> 00:35:58.043
And I'm thinking, man, I saw
you at the frickin' hibachi,

00:35:59.030 --> 00:36:00.710
at like seven o'clock.

00:36:00.710 --> 00:36:02.570
- Yeah you can't be here.
- And now it's 1 a.m.

00:36:02.570 --> 00:36:03.620
and you're here.

00:36:03.620 --> 00:36:04.600
- Right.

00:36:04.600 --> 00:36:07.800
- And I'm in third grade, it's so weird.

00:36:07.800 --> 00:36:09.970
That was excellent news but
as we were waiting for him

00:36:09.970 --> 00:36:11.963
to do the scope, Mom and I had to step out

00:36:11.963 --> 00:36:13.640
and just wait and it was like

00:36:14.860 --> 00:36:17.810
we were sitting in the waiting
room just the two of us,

00:36:17.810 --> 00:36:19.060
no one else was in there.

00:36:19.990 --> 00:36:23.890
And we didn't say a word.

00:36:23.890 --> 00:36:25.900
We were so exhausted.

00:36:25.900 --> 00:36:27.593
- It's like somebody who's
been through a battle,

00:36:27.593 --> 00:36:29.572
you just sit there.

00:36:29.572 --> 00:36:33.240
- You just sit there
and it was very surreal.

00:36:33.240 --> 00:36:35.870
And she told me that it
really hadn't sunken in

00:36:35.870 --> 00:36:37.460
at that point how dire things were,

00:36:37.460 --> 00:36:38.960
she was in that mode and...

00:36:41.360 --> 00:36:45.020
We're kind of in fight
mode, stay positive mode

00:36:45.020 --> 00:36:48.321
and so at that point
it was very touch and,

00:36:48.321 --> 00:36:51.360
the great news was they
found the bleeding,

00:36:51.360 --> 00:36:53.860
they had stopped the
bleeding, they didn't have to

00:36:55.600 --> 00:36:58.380
do a second procedure which
could have damaged the kidneys

00:36:58.380 --> 00:37:00.650
in order to try to find the bleeding

00:37:00.650 --> 00:37:02.890
or a third procedure worst case

00:37:02.890 --> 00:37:05.050
where they had to do surgery.

00:37:05.050 --> 00:37:08.180
The surgeons were coming in
and prepping him for that too

00:37:08.180 --> 00:37:09.013
in case they had to.

00:37:09.013 --> 00:37:14.000
It was that, everybody was buzzing around

00:37:15.510 --> 00:37:19.040
and it was, yeah, it was very kinetic

00:37:19.040 --> 00:37:22.793
and it was real, man.

00:37:23.900 --> 00:37:28.900
And by that morning, he had
stabilized and of course,

00:37:30.730 --> 00:37:33.100
he wasn't given any drink or food

00:37:33.100 --> 00:37:36.860
for probably the next
48 hours as it turns out

00:37:36.860 --> 00:37:37.764
but they were monitoring--

00:37:37.764 --> 00:37:41.010
- They were hydrating him intravenously.

00:37:41.010 --> 00:37:42.670
- Yeah.
- Or else he would die.

00:37:42.670 --> 00:37:44.950
- And giving him medicine for the ulcers

00:37:44.950 --> 00:37:47.683
and giving him medicine to help,

00:37:48.840 --> 00:37:53.023
to preserve them from bleeding
and giving him more blood.

00:37:55.233 --> 00:38:00.120
To come out to a total
of 14 units, 14 pints.

00:38:00.120 --> 00:38:01.253
- So again,

00:38:02.660 --> 00:38:05.390
he replaced his blood supply.

00:38:05.390 --> 00:38:06.670
- Yeah.

00:38:06.670 --> 00:38:08.658
- He completely replaced his blood supply.

00:38:08.658 --> 00:38:09.533
- Wild.

00:38:10.620 --> 00:38:13.090
And so then--
- Everybody needs that.

00:38:13.090 --> 00:38:15.400
It's just like changing
your transmission fluid,

00:38:15.400 --> 00:38:16.281
every once in awhile.

00:38:16.281 --> 00:38:18.770
- That's literally what happened.

00:38:18.770 --> 00:38:21.590
But they didn't drain him
out and then fill him up,

00:38:21.590 --> 00:38:24.570
it was a constant flow, it was more--

00:38:24.570 --> 00:38:28.380
- Yeah, you can't do that with a human.

00:38:28.380 --> 00:38:30.280
- It was a nightmare.
- You can do that with a car.

00:38:30.280 --> 00:38:32.453
- It was an absolute nightmare and then,

00:38:33.830 --> 00:38:36.270
so then for the next two
days it's kinda touch and go

00:38:36.270 --> 00:38:39.850
to make sure that he's not bleeding

00:38:39.850 --> 00:38:41.050
and even though he's not eating,

00:38:41.050 --> 00:38:46.050
then as you slowly introduce
drinking and then eating foods,

00:38:47.420 --> 00:38:49.763
clear fluids and all that jazz.

00:38:50.760 --> 00:38:53.563
It's just continue to monitor
that he's not bleeding.

00:38:54.840 --> 00:38:59.840
And let's see, so we checked
him in Saturday morning.

00:38:59.980 --> 00:39:02.760
We checked him out by the end of the week

00:39:02.760 --> 00:39:04.963
so he was there a whole
week so then it was,

00:39:06.600 --> 00:39:09.760
it basically shifted
from emergency situation,

00:39:09.760 --> 00:39:12.494
he left the ICU to a regular hospital room

00:39:12.494 --> 00:39:15.020
which then you and Jessie
came that first night

00:39:15.020 --> 00:39:17.163
and visited him there which was great.

00:39:18.304 --> 00:39:23.200
But then it was just, that's
when it really started

00:39:23.200 --> 00:39:24.290
to hit us that

00:39:27.020 --> 00:39:31.560
there's only three people to
support him in his recovery

00:39:31.560 --> 00:39:35.990
as he's here and that proved to be

00:39:35.990 --> 00:39:38.750
very challenging because my
mom stayed there all night

00:39:38.750 --> 00:39:40.700
and then as soon as I could
get up in the morning,

00:39:40.700 --> 00:39:44.220
I would go and relieve her.

00:39:44.220 --> 00:39:46.470
Either Christy would come
get her or I'd send her home

00:39:46.470 --> 00:39:48.190
to my house in an Uber to take a shower

00:39:48.190 --> 00:39:50.290
and try to sleep for a few hours and then,

00:39:52.340 --> 00:39:55.040
Christy and Mom would
come and then I would go

00:39:55.040 --> 00:39:57.000
into work for a few hours and then

00:39:57.000 --> 00:40:01.710
so someone was always there
with him for the whole week.

00:40:01.710 --> 00:40:06.250
And I really gained an appreciation
for how important it is

00:40:06.250 --> 00:40:07.820
to have people come visit.

00:40:07.820 --> 00:40:10.050
When you guys came that night to visit,

00:40:10.050 --> 00:40:12.985
it was a tremendous
help and something that

00:40:12.985 --> 00:40:17.250
I never appreciated when it's like,

00:40:17.250 --> 00:40:19.720
someone's in the hospital sick,
well you should go see 'em,

00:40:19.720 --> 00:40:21.550
you know, you should go see 'em

00:40:21.550 --> 00:40:24.100
because it's the right thing to do.

00:40:24.100 --> 00:40:27.290
But I never really connected
the dots that for the people

00:40:27.290 --> 00:40:30.240
who are actively supporting
the person in recovery,

00:40:30.240 --> 00:40:32.383
it's also a huge help to them to,

00:40:33.680 --> 00:40:35.460
you run out of stuff to talk about.

00:40:35.460 --> 00:40:39.620
You run out of, it
drains all of your energy

00:40:39.620 --> 00:40:42.393
to sit in a hospital room.

00:40:43.370 --> 00:40:47.240
And when doctors and nurses are comin' in

00:40:47.240 --> 00:40:50.703
and just constantly pricking,
prodding and doing stuff,

00:40:51.750 --> 00:40:54.900
it's not a happy, it's
not Disneyland, you know.

00:40:54.900 --> 00:40:57.085
- No and as a matter of fact--
- My takeaway.

00:40:57.085 --> 00:41:01.083
- We were there for a
very short period of time,

00:41:02.520 --> 00:41:05.003
but first of all,

00:41:06.230 --> 00:41:08.853
I got lost.

00:41:09.730 --> 00:41:13.430
I had a window of time
between leaving here

00:41:13.430 --> 00:41:15.140
and then going to therapy

00:41:15.140 --> 00:41:19.690
and I had kinda planned
to have half an hour

00:41:19.690 --> 00:41:21.829
and Jessie was gonna meet me.

00:41:21.829 --> 00:41:26.480
But my frickin' GPS, my phone, sent me to

00:41:27.680 --> 00:41:29.400
the back of the hospital.

00:41:29.400 --> 00:41:30.807
- Yeah and it's a big hospital.

00:41:30.807 --> 00:41:33.030
- And I parked and then I had to,

00:41:33.030 --> 00:41:35.233
I was like 20 minutes from my car.

00:41:36.190 --> 00:41:40.920
And then I walk into the
lobby, the hospital lobby,

00:41:40.920 --> 00:41:44.620
and I see you checking
in and you had left work

00:41:44.620 --> 00:41:46.670
like way after me.
- Oh yeah.

00:41:46.670 --> 00:41:48.910
- And that's when I see through the window

00:41:48.910 --> 00:41:51.850
and I see the thing I had been
looking for the whole time,

00:41:51.850 --> 00:41:54.520
the grand hospital entrance.

00:41:54.520 --> 00:41:58.730
There's multiple doors and
there's a big horseshoe

00:41:58.730 --> 00:42:01.190
where you kind of drop
people off and I saw Jessie

00:42:01.190 --> 00:42:02.240
and I was like, "Where'd you park?"

00:42:02.240 --> 00:42:04.210
She was like, "I valeted." (chuckles)

00:42:04.210 --> 00:42:05.630
- Yeah she went to the right entrance.

00:42:05.630 --> 00:42:07.940
- So yeah I got lost.

00:42:07.940 --> 00:42:09.920
- And I'm so sorry that
happened to you, man.

00:42:09.920 --> 00:42:12.000
That's kind of like
having a bleeding ulcer

00:42:12.000 --> 00:42:13.580
and almost dying.
- Yeah yeah yeah.

00:42:13.580 --> 00:42:17.080
But what I was getting to is how much

00:42:18.880 --> 00:42:22.720
I was just reminded how
much I hate hospitals.

00:42:22.720 --> 00:42:25.810
Now, I appreciate hospitals.

00:42:25.810 --> 00:42:27.980
They're absolutely necessary.

00:42:27.980 --> 00:42:30.270
But I get, and this is
probably something a lot

00:42:30.270 --> 00:42:32.330
of people relate to, it's
like, I've never been

00:42:32.330 --> 00:42:34.320
in a hospital personally,
like I've never had

00:42:34.320 --> 00:42:35.870
to go to the hospital for anything.

00:42:35.870 --> 00:42:36.703
- Mm-hmm.

00:42:36.703 --> 00:42:40.850
- I've been to the doctor or
have had very small procedures

00:42:40.850 --> 00:42:42.573
at a outpatient clinic.

00:42:43.640 --> 00:42:45.484
But never been in a
hospital and when I go into

00:42:45.484 --> 00:42:48.970
a hospital and on the
floor that Lewis that on,

00:42:48.970 --> 00:42:53.600
you had to pass by several
rooms that were open

00:42:53.600 --> 00:42:57.250
and these people are in
different states of disrepair.

00:42:57.250 --> 00:42:58.710
- Yeah.

00:42:58.710 --> 00:43:00.310
Sometimes there's screaming.

00:43:00.310 --> 00:43:02.260
- Well there was one guy who was having

00:43:03.163 --> 00:43:05.470
an interesting exchange with a nurse.

00:43:05.470 --> 00:43:09.390
- Yeah, there's interesting
scents that float about.

00:43:09.390 --> 00:43:11.820
- And again, I don't know what it is.

00:43:11.820 --> 00:43:15.230
I was going to therapy right after that.

00:43:15.230 --> 00:43:19.310
I didn't get into this but I don't know,

00:43:19.310 --> 00:43:20.780
I just get this feeling that I'm just like

00:43:20.780 --> 00:43:22.890
I don't want to be here and I don't want

00:43:22.890 --> 00:43:24.700
to be a patient here ever.

00:43:24.700 --> 00:43:27.820
- Yeah and you feel like
being here might make me sick.

00:43:27.820 --> 00:43:30.250
I think things like that.

00:43:30.250 --> 00:43:31.310
But--
- And there's people

00:43:31.310 --> 00:43:34.510
who have to spend not just--
- Oh gosh.

00:43:34.510 --> 00:43:39.070
- We met the kids at
St. Jude who basically,

00:43:39.070 --> 00:43:40.550
some of them have spent--
- They live there.

00:43:40.550 --> 00:43:43.210
- The majority of their
lives in that environment.

00:43:43.210 --> 00:43:46.380
And places like St. Jude
do go to incredible lengths

00:43:46.380 --> 00:43:48.343
to create an environment that is not,

00:43:50.565 --> 00:43:54.500
it's not depressing other than the fact

00:43:54.500 --> 00:43:55.460
that why you're there.

00:43:55.460 --> 00:43:58.330
- Yeah and this hospital
was a great facility.

00:43:58.330 --> 00:44:01.950
There's no actual criticism
that we could level

00:44:01.950 --> 00:44:06.420
on the facility or the
faculty at any point.

00:44:06.420 --> 00:44:10.040
It's just, it's more about
the stigma associated

00:44:10.040 --> 00:44:14.570
with hospitals or the fact
that a hospital is still,

00:44:14.570 --> 00:44:19.570
it is what it is and it's
not a pleasant environment.

00:44:19.740 --> 00:44:22.680
- And it's way more pleasant
now than it was, what--

00:44:22.680 --> 00:44:24.800
- Oh gosh.
- 50 years ago or whatever.

00:44:24.800 --> 00:44:27.360
- I mean all the procedures
they were doing on Lewis,

00:44:27.360 --> 00:44:28.760
they would cart the stuff into the room

00:44:28.760 --> 00:44:30.789
and do the scope and everything
there, it was amazing.

00:44:30.789 --> 00:44:32.680
I was talking to him later.

00:44:32.680 --> 00:44:34.530
We were watching Leave
It to Beaver one morning,

00:44:34.530 --> 00:44:38.730
I was like can you imagine back in the day

00:44:38.730 --> 00:44:41.560
if Beaver had some bleeding ulcers,

00:44:41.560 --> 00:44:43.145
what would they have done?

00:44:43.145 --> 00:44:44.980
- He would be dead, they'd bury Beaver.

00:44:44.980 --> 00:44:48.040
- They just would have
done surgery in order

00:44:48.040 --> 00:44:50.853
to find where you were bleeding, I think.

00:44:50.853 --> 00:44:52.440
- Mm-hmm.

00:44:52.440 --> 00:44:55.150
- There's no scoping in your
room and 30 minutes later,

00:44:55.150 --> 00:44:58.250
your wife can walk back
in and get good news

00:44:58.250 --> 00:44:59.590
and a picture of it.

00:44:59.590 --> 00:45:00.650
- It's all conjecture.

00:45:00.650 --> 00:45:03.650
- But I was, I gained
such an appreciation,

00:45:03.650 --> 00:45:06.830
like I said, for the nurses
and hospital staff in general

00:45:07.850 --> 00:45:11.410
but also a revived sense
of how important it is

00:45:11.410 --> 00:45:15.550
to visit people in the
hospital and not let the way

00:45:15.550 --> 00:45:17.330
that we instinctively think about it,

00:45:17.330 --> 00:45:21.250
like I should visit but I'm not going to,

00:45:21.250 --> 00:45:24.343
it makes a huge, huge difference,
and when you guys came,

00:45:25.763 --> 00:45:28.980
you lighten the mood, have
somebody else to talk to,

00:45:28.980 --> 00:45:30.373
pass a little bit of time.

00:45:31.210 --> 00:45:36.210
And obviously also give
and receive love and care.

00:45:37.731 --> 00:45:40.530
It can't be understated
but for some reason

00:45:40.530 --> 00:45:42.330
in my mind I think it
had gone to that place

00:45:42.330 --> 00:45:43.380
and you know what?

00:45:43.380 --> 00:45:45.724
In a few days we're comin' up
on the two year anniversary

00:45:45.724 --> 00:45:49.863
of Lily having her spinal fusion surgery.

00:45:51.090 --> 00:45:53.930
As we record this it will
be this coming Friday

00:45:53.930 --> 00:45:56.120
'cause I've put it on the calendar.

00:45:56.120 --> 00:45:57.570
Two years.

00:45:57.570 --> 00:46:00.210
And I'm a little discouraged
that I'd forgotten

00:46:01.590 --> 00:46:03.100
what I'd learned there,

00:46:03.100 --> 00:46:04.750
the things that I'm now talking about.

00:46:04.750 --> 00:46:07.050
Because in her recovery
how important it was

00:46:07.050 --> 00:46:09.223
for people to come and offer support.

00:46:11.080 --> 00:46:14.040
And again, I just felt
for Lewis and for Mom,

00:46:14.040 --> 00:46:16.180
especially Lewis because
his family wasn't there

00:46:16.180 --> 00:46:18.330
and it was an interesting
situation because

00:46:20.160 --> 00:46:22.710
I'm in a position where I'm
spending hours with this guy

00:46:22.710 --> 00:46:25.810
that I've never spent,
I'll ride on the Gator

00:46:25.810 --> 00:46:29.160
with him and the kids down and
see the cows and come back,

00:46:29.160 --> 00:46:31.670
but it's not like the two of
us have a vibrant relationship

00:46:31.670 --> 00:46:32.763
where we hang out.

00:46:33.640 --> 00:46:38.640
I love him because he loves
my mom and my mom loves him

00:46:39.480 --> 00:46:41.100
and they are very happy together

00:46:41.100 --> 00:46:44.510
and they have a beautiful relationship

00:46:44.510 --> 00:46:46.050
and they're perfect for each other.

00:46:46.050 --> 00:46:50.530
That is enough for me
to love him unabashedly.

00:46:50.530 --> 00:46:52.171
But it--
- The Gator helps.

00:46:52.171 --> 00:46:53.910
The Gator helps.
- The Gator helps.

00:46:53.910 --> 00:46:56.643
But when it comes to the
experience of a relationship,

00:46:59.560 --> 00:47:01.560
it was an interesting position to be in.

00:47:02.570 --> 00:47:04.263
For him and for me probably.

00:47:06.170 --> 00:47:10.250
Where we have to assume
the role of the people

00:47:10.250 --> 00:47:15.250
who are providing the
only emotional support

00:47:15.270 --> 00:47:17.163
for hours at a time.

00:47:19.120 --> 00:47:22.863
And I really had to deal just honestly.

00:47:25.320 --> 00:47:28.580
This is not about Lewis and my
relationship with him really,

00:47:28.580 --> 00:47:31.190
I think it just kinda set the
stage for me to think more

00:47:31.190 --> 00:47:36.190
about my level of selfishness.

00:47:36.420 --> 00:47:39.480
Honestly a lot of that came up because

00:47:42.740 --> 00:47:46.520
I just realized how much
I want my life to be

00:47:47.610 --> 00:47:49.690
ordered the way I want it to be.

00:47:49.690 --> 00:47:51.550
Well they're gonna come in for two days,

00:47:51.550 --> 00:47:54.210
this is our itinerary, we're
gonna have a really good time,

00:47:54.210 --> 00:47:56.180
and then they're gonna leave
and get back to their cows

00:47:56.180 --> 00:47:58.610
where they wanna be and
everybody's gonna be happy

00:47:58.610 --> 00:48:02.370
and I know what my next
week has in store for me.

00:48:02.370 --> 00:48:04.800
And I'm looking forward to it.

00:48:04.800 --> 00:48:06.273
It's a light week at work.

00:48:07.369 --> 00:48:09.850
I was looking forward to specific things.

00:48:09.850 --> 00:48:10.683
- Mm-hmm.

00:48:14.120 --> 00:48:16.863
- We weren't filming anything so it was,

00:48:17.860 --> 00:48:20.820
that was actually a blessing because I--

00:48:20.820 --> 00:48:23.250
- Yeah.
- And again, I actually,

00:48:23.250 --> 00:48:25.270
with all the stuff that
we have going on at work,

00:48:25.270 --> 00:48:28.920
I actually had a week where pretty easily

00:48:28.920 --> 00:48:31.750
I was readily available
to support in a way

00:48:31.750 --> 00:48:33.810
that I very much needed to be there.

00:48:33.810 --> 00:48:34.960
- Which is very unusual.

00:48:34.960 --> 00:48:38.203
- Yeah, and I'm very grateful for that.

00:48:39.050 --> 00:48:40.480
I would like to think
that I would have made

00:48:40.480 --> 00:48:42.990
the right decision to
change whatever I was doing

00:48:42.990 --> 00:48:45.440
in order to be there anyway.

00:48:45.440 --> 00:48:50.440
But I don't know if I would
have made the right decision,

00:48:50.870 --> 00:48:55.140
because I was still wrestling
with the selfishness

00:48:55.140 --> 00:48:58.910
of my plans, my life, my time,

00:48:58.910 --> 00:49:01.383
my desires, my comfort, you know?

00:49:03.650 --> 00:49:06.790
Again, it's embarrassing to talk about

00:49:06.790 --> 00:49:09.540
but I feel like that's part of my takeaway

00:49:09.540 --> 00:49:11.940
in terms of my growth in this situation is

00:49:13.730 --> 00:49:16.230
what really is love in this situation?

00:49:16.230 --> 00:49:19.363
It's making a decision to be there.

00:49:20.240 --> 00:49:25.240
Aspiring to be better at
loving somebody that I do love

00:49:25.930 --> 00:49:27.823
and knowing that I just didn't,

00:49:29.240 --> 00:49:31.990
that I had so many
competing personal desires

00:49:31.990 --> 00:49:36.373
that were just lame, you know?

00:49:38.700 --> 00:49:41.150
Sure, yeah, it's difficult
to go through that.

00:49:41.150 --> 00:49:43.490
It's difficult to hang out at a hospital.

00:49:43.490 --> 00:49:45.880
It's difficult to know what to talk about.

00:49:45.880 --> 00:49:49.620
But really, these are things
that in your own brain

00:49:49.620 --> 00:49:51.220
you would complain about?

00:49:51.220 --> 00:49:52.470
- Well I think everybody,

00:49:53.590 --> 00:49:55.293
I think most people would.

00:49:56.395 --> 00:50:01.395
There are some people who are naturally,

00:50:01.980 --> 00:50:06.480
my wife being one of them,
naturally equipped to just,

00:50:06.480 --> 00:50:11.480
she can walk into the
hospital room with Lewis

00:50:11.540 --> 00:50:15.470
and she A, she knows exactly what to say.

00:50:15.470 --> 00:50:17.510
- She knows what to say
and she knows what to do.

00:50:17.510 --> 00:50:20.310
- And she could talk to somebody forever.

00:50:20.310 --> 00:50:23.620
I come in and I'm like,
"Welcome to California."

00:50:23.620 --> 00:50:25.970
I make a joke, right?

00:50:25.970 --> 00:50:28.900
- Which is helpful but yeah it's kind of,

00:50:28.900 --> 00:50:31.970
I relate to that 'cause
it's like searching for

00:50:31.970 --> 00:50:34.000
how did I get myself in this situation?

00:50:34.000 --> 00:50:35.350
- Well and a lot of times--

00:50:36.373 --> 00:50:39.930
- I don't know what to do here
and my instincts don't apply.

00:50:39.930 --> 00:50:43.883
- Well and I've actually,
in many different ways,

00:50:45.000 --> 00:50:47.763
this is one of the
advantages and I don't know,

00:50:48.620 --> 00:50:50.780
advantages and disadvantages
of being married

00:50:50.780 --> 00:50:54.790
to someone who complements
you in a certain way, right?

00:50:54.790 --> 00:50:57.670
Who has a characteristic, for me,

00:50:57.670 --> 00:51:02.510
I can just be like, oh,
Jessie is really good at that.

00:51:02.510 --> 00:51:04.630
She knows what to say, she
knows how to comfort somebody,

00:51:04.630 --> 00:51:09.460
so the unit of Rhett and
Jessie can comfort someone

00:51:09.460 --> 00:51:12.970
because Jessie will do
it because I can't do it,

00:51:12.970 --> 00:51:15.590
and for me, it's part
of what you're saying

00:51:15.590 --> 00:51:19.399
which is just like well I got
too much stuff I gotta get to.

00:51:19.399 --> 00:51:21.420
My stuff that I gotta worry about's

00:51:21.420 --> 00:51:24.530
more important than this,
there's part of it that's that

00:51:24.530 --> 00:51:28.780
and then the other side is
just feeling inadequate.

00:51:28.780 --> 00:51:31.870
- And also what happens
when the comforting aspect

00:51:31.870 --> 00:51:34.014
of the unit is the one
that needs comforting?

00:51:34.014 --> 00:51:36.750
(chuckles) It's like it
kinda breaks down internally

00:51:36.750 --> 00:51:37.810
at that point too, right?

00:51:37.810 --> 00:51:39.209
- Yeah, that's when you hire someone.

00:51:39.209 --> 00:51:40.309
- (chuckles) Oh shoot.

00:51:41.550 --> 00:51:44.630
- No, yeah, exactly.

00:51:44.630 --> 00:51:47.300
That's where it becomes,
it's not a good thing.

00:51:47.300 --> 00:51:51.380
I'm saying you end up creating
a, your deficit can grow

00:51:51.380 --> 00:51:53.680
because you're relying
on someone to fill that.

00:51:53.680 --> 00:51:57.140
- And I learned so much
from Christy to be able

00:51:57.140 --> 00:52:02.140
to meet people's needs and have empathy.

00:52:02.350 --> 00:52:04.610
I guess that's what we're
talking about, right?

00:52:04.610 --> 00:52:07.130
We're talking about being empathetic.

00:52:07.130 --> 00:52:08.840
- Yeah, I think that's a part of it.

00:52:08.840 --> 00:52:13.840
- And again, that's why I'm so in awe

00:52:14.220 --> 00:52:18.440
of people who devote
their lives in the field

00:52:18.440 --> 00:52:23.200
of nursing and all the people who would,

00:52:23.200 --> 00:52:25.300
the people who come in and clean the room.

00:52:29.600 --> 00:52:31.580
The assistants to the nurses,

00:52:31.580 --> 00:52:36.580
everybody who have devoted
their lives to caring for people

00:52:36.820 --> 00:52:39.360
in that way is just...

00:52:42.825 --> 00:52:43.658
It's amazing.

00:52:45.860 --> 00:52:50.860
And it draws into focus ways
that I can kinda step up

00:52:50.970 --> 00:52:53.380
and be a better person, I think.

00:52:53.380 --> 00:52:56.240
I think in watching those people and

00:52:59.430 --> 00:53:01.880
dealing with the patients
and even in the moments

00:53:01.880 --> 00:53:06.010
of frustration and in
confusion, just having composure

00:53:06.010 --> 00:53:07.250
and still having...

00:53:13.010 --> 00:53:16.326
Just bringing healing to
a situation is something

00:53:16.326 --> 00:53:19.833
that I don't know, my eyes
were really opened to that.

00:53:22.010 --> 00:53:23.300
Lily's situation was different

00:53:23.300 --> 00:53:26.350
because it was a planned procedure

00:53:26.350 --> 00:53:31.113
and it was a controlled recovery
that we were prepared for.

00:53:32.130 --> 00:53:37.130
So there was never any huge
scrambling emergency moment.

00:53:37.890 --> 00:53:39.530
I think that's the difference here

00:53:39.530 --> 00:53:42.480
that then there were ugly aspects to it,

00:53:42.480 --> 00:53:45.630
to this experience that

00:53:46.890 --> 00:53:49.770
I witnessed firsthand
people stepping up and

00:53:52.260 --> 00:53:53.310
helping.

00:53:53.310 --> 00:53:58.310
- Well and what it makes me
think about is people who...

00:54:03.250 --> 00:54:04.930
Lewis, he's home now, right?

00:54:04.930 --> 00:54:08.987
So it was an incredibly
difficult week full of lessons,

00:54:11.640 --> 00:54:14.213
but what if the second week
was just like the first week

00:54:14.213 --> 00:54:16.820
and the third week was
just like the second week?

00:54:16.820 --> 00:54:20.880
And people who have their
lives turned upside down

00:54:20.880 --> 00:54:25.880
because someone becomes
critically ill, terminally ill,

00:54:25.880 --> 00:54:27.579
like if it happens to
somebody in your family,

00:54:27.579 --> 00:54:29.216
if it happens to one of your children,

00:54:29.216 --> 00:54:32.210
then all of a sudden your
life basically becomes

00:54:32.210 --> 00:54:35.010
what you're talking about.
- Mm-hmm.

00:54:35.010 --> 00:54:38.763
- And that is, that
becomes your new normal.

00:54:40.400 --> 00:54:43.680
Which again, I'm gonna
keep plugging St. Jude.

00:54:43.680 --> 00:54:48.669
I didn't expect to but
they helped us understand--

00:54:48.669 --> 00:54:49.950
- Right.

00:54:49.950 --> 00:54:53.090
- All the support that a family needs.

00:54:53.090 --> 00:54:56.460
They have a program at St. Jude

00:54:56.460 --> 00:54:59.157
that is specifically designed for siblings

00:54:59.157 --> 00:55:04.157
of terminally ill kids and
because what they've seen is that

00:55:04.330 --> 00:55:07.360
these kids, they have emotional needs too.

00:55:07.360 --> 00:55:08.920
- Yeah.
- And when all the attention

00:55:08.920 --> 00:55:13.230
and all the resources are going
on this person who is sick,

00:55:13.230 --> 00:55:14.970
what happens to you?

00:55:14.970 --> 00:55:18.523
I think in the book and the movie Wonder,

00:55:20.714 --> 00:55:24.850
they talk about the older sister's story.

00:55:24.850 --> 00:55:28.160
Anyway, all that to say St. Jude is sort

00:55:28.160 --> 00:55:30.130
of like love in action.

00:55:30.130 --> 00:55:33.200
You're talking about what
you can do personally

00:55:34.320 --> 00:55:36.210
and they've kinda created a whole system

00:55:36.210 --> 00:55:38.253
to support that kind of love in action.

00:55:39.330 --> 00:55:41.053
- This experience I think is,

00:55:42.320 --> 00:55:47.050
it's just kinda jostled me awake to my

00:55:49.960 --> 00:55:54.700
unwarranted expectations
that my life is gonna be,

00:55:54.700 --> 00:55:58.623
continue to be what I expect it to be.

00:56:02.970 --> 00:56:06.290
We experience so much positive things

00:56:06.290 --> 00:56:10.280
and it's like, in this conversation,

00:56:10.280 --> 00:56:11.860
you start to feel a
little guilty about it,

00:56:11.860 --> 00:56:15.003
but it's not that I wanna feel
guilty, I wanna be grateful.

00:56:17.050 --> 00:56:19.940
But I also want not to live my life as if

00:56:19.940 --> 00:56:22.760
I expect or demand

00:56:24.410 --> 00:56:27.080
no curve balls, you know?

00:56:27.080 --> 00:56:29.110
- Well yeah.

00:56:29.110 --> 00:56:30.610
And we talked about this before.

00:56:30.610 --> 00:56:33.610
There are many times when
things are going well

00:56:35.010 --> 00:56:39.800
and I think both of our personalities

00:56:39.800 --> 00:56:42.623
are actually geared towards all right,

00:56:44.140 --> 00:56:46.300
kind of expecting something to go wrong.

00:56:46.300 --> 00:56:49.660
Because things shouldn't,
things are going well right now

00:56:49.660 --> 00:56:52.500
so surely the bottom's
gonna drop out somewhere

00:56:53.870 --> 00:56:55.087
and sort of just anticipating that

00:56:55.087 --> 00:56:56.970
something like that will happen but also

00:56:56.970 --> 00:57:01.840
at the same time knowing I
am not emotionally prepared

00:57:01.840 --> 00:57:05.828
and I am also not, I am
also too selfish right now

00:57:05.828 --> 00:57:08.090
to be able to take this,

00:57:08.090 --> 00:57:11.150
something that would
inconvenience me to that level.

00:57:11.150 --> 00:57:11.983
- Yeah.

00:57:13.239 --> 00:57:18.239
I'll share one more image,
something that I wanna,

00:57:20.070 --> 00:57:24.760
a memory that I wanna stick
with me I'll share with you

00:57:24.760 --> 00:57:28.640
in order to help solidify
it since sometimes

00:57:28.640 --> 00:57:31.050
maybe saying stories
helps me remember them

00:57:31.050 --> 00:57:33.150
or at least now I'll have an audio record.

00:57:37.250 --> 00:57:39.510
Oh gosh, we must have been three days in.

00:57:39.510 --> 00:57:41.900
They had started allowing him to drink

00:57:41.900 --> 00:57:46.900
and eat some liquified foods
but then they had evidence

00:57:48.000 --> 00:57:50.653
that he might have been bleeding again.

00:57:52.520 --> 00:57:54.480
It was kind of a panic
mode for a few minutes

00:57:54.480 --> 00:57:56.570
and then they pulled,
well not a few minutes,

00:57:56.570 --> 00:58:00.100
but longer than that,
they decided to pull back

00:58:00.100 --> 00:58:02.900
and he's not allowed to
eat or drink anything

00:58:02.900 --> 00:58:04.650
as they monitored him.

00:58:04.650 --> 00:58:08.360
Now it turns out that he
was not bleeding anymore

00:58:08.360 --> 00:58:12.193
but that was a setback in
terms of his experience.

00:58:13.360 --> 00:58:16.920
You're confined in a hospital
on the opposite coast

00:58:18.320 --> 00:58:22.790
with me supporting you every
morning alone. (chuckles)

00:58:22.790 --> 00:58:26.000
No, he was very gracious
but it was very frustrating

00:58:27.540 --> 00:58:31.203
to not even be able to drink water at all.

00:58:34.740 --> 00:58:36.200
It was tough to keep his spirits up

00:58:36.200 --> 00:58:40.450
and the television helped.

00:58:40.450 --> 00:58:44.780
I know at home, he falls
asleep to the television on.

00:58:44.780 --> 00:58:48.350
That's like his white
noise so that helped him.

00:58:48.350 --> 00:58:51.380
It gets him rest, and then,

00:58:51.380 --> 00:58:53.630
but the thing that
happened a few hours later

00:58:53.630 --> 00:58:55.500
after the step backwards,

00:58:55.500 --> 00:58:57.230
not being able to even drink anything

00:58:57.230 --> 00:58:59.703
and him literally being parched.

00:59:02.530 --> 00:59:06.137
The television went just to fuzz.

00:59:06.137 --> 00:59:07.530
(mimics static crackling)

00:59:07.530 --> 00:59:08.783
And I look at it.

00:59:09.700 --> 00:59:13.593
He didn't say a word but
I'm thinking oh my gosh,

00:59:14.460 --> 00:59:19.450
this is like the main
thing that feels like

00:59:19.450 --> 00:59:21.540
holding us together now
is just the television.

00:59:21.540 --> 00:59:24.520
There's no food, there's
nobody else but me to talk to.

00:59:24.520 --> 00:59:27.940
And now the television is weak signal.

00:59:27.940 --> 00:59:30.520
It said like signal cannot be acquired.

00:59:30.520 --> 00:59:31.763
I'm like oh crap.

00:59:32.870 --> 00:59:36.850
And then I'm panicking
inside, I'm trying to put

00:59:36.850 --> 00:59:41.850
on a positive face and I'm
like, well, what do we do now?

00:59:44.550 --> 00:59:48.560
And then I stood up and
I look at the television,

00:59:48.560 --> 00:59:50.300
kinda like when you open
the hood of your car

00:59:50.300 --> 00:59:52.770
when you know something's wrong with it

00:59:52.770 --> 00:59:54.210
and you're supposed to look at the motor

00:59:54.210 --> 00:59:56.020
before you decide to get
someone who's qualified

00:59:56.020 --> 00:59:58.710
to fix it, I'm like, uh, I'm
gonna look at the television.

00:59:58.710 --> 01:00:00.420
What do we do now, and
so I'm standing there

01:00:00.420 --> 01:00:04.103
at the foot of the bed
and then I was like,

01:00:05.617 --> 01:00:08.700
"It looks like I'm your
only form of entertainment."

01:00:08.700 --> 01:00:10.463
And then I just started doing,

01:00:11.560 --> 01:00:13.923
I guess I'll call it an Irish jig.

01:00:15.620 --> 01:00:17.880
It was kind of a Riverdance thing.

01:00:17.880 --> 01:00:20.040
There was some elbow movement though

01:00:20.040 --> 01:00:22.373
and a lot of leg movements
and some kicking.

01:00:23.530 --> 01:00:24.780
- How long did that last?

01:00:25.902 --> 01:00:27.093
- 'Til I got winded.

01:00:28.410 --> 01:00:30.850
'Til I got a smile out of him.

01:00:30.850 --> 01:00:31.993
And then I was like,

01:00:35.027 --> 01:00:37.710
"Do you have any cash?"

01:00:37.710 --> 01:00:40.157
He said, "No, I don't have any.

01:00:40.157 --> 01:00:42.397
"I don't know where my
wallet is," and I was like,

01:00:42.397 --> 01:00:44.530
"Well, then I'm not gonna strip for ya,"

01:00:44.530 --> 01:00:47.780
and then I got a laugh out of
him and it was the first time

01:00:47.780 --> 01:00:52.543
he had laughed, I don't
know, since before he landed.

01:00:55.688 --> 01:00:58.170
And then so that helped,

01:00:58.170 --> 01:01:01.170
but then I ended up
unplugging the television

01:01:01.170 --> 01:01:05.930
and doing all sorts of religious
ceremonies in my own brain

01:01:05.930 --> 01:01:08.620
and plugging it back in
and it started working.

01:01:08.620 --> 01:01:10.840
So we got Leave It to Beaver back on

01:01:10.840 --> 01:01:12.123
and some Price Is Right.

01:01:14.780 --> 01:01:17.080
And then they started introducing,

01:01:17.080 --> 01:01:21.060
allowing him to eat,
drink water, and then,

01:01:21.060 --> 01:01:24.570
eat these Icees, like lemon ices.

01:01:24.570 --> 01:01:26.360
But he had been talking
about these lemon ices

01:01:26.360 --> 01:01:27.193
'cause they had let him have 'em

01:01:27.193 --> 01:01:28.800
and then they took 'em away.

01:01:28.800 --> 01:01:30.610
And then a whole day later,

01:01:30.610 --> 01:01:32.430
he's talking about these lemon ices.

01:01:32.430 --> 01:01:34.610
- Anything at that point.
- Oh gosh.

01:01:34.610 --> 01:01:39.410
It was like a mirage in
the room, I felt like.

01:01:39.410 --> 01:01:43.360
And they finally told
him he could have one.

01:01:43.360 --> 01:01:46.830
And I brought it in there and
this was like a culmination

01:01:46.830 --> 01:01:49.210
of like hours and hours
of being fixated on it

01:01:49.210 --> 01:01:51.830
and then he had this
lemon ice and he's eating.

01:01:51.830 --> 01:01:55.160
I'm like, "How's that lemon ice?"

01:01:55.160 --> 01:01:57.197
He was like, "Oh.

01:01:57.197 --> 01:01:59.237
"It's good, good, good."

01:01:59.237 --> 01:02:00.314
(Rhett laughs)

01:02:00.314 --> 01:02:02.360
And I said, "What did you say?"

01:02:02.360 --> 01:02:04.430
He said, "I like that lemon ice."

01:02:04.430 --> 01:02:06.400
I was like, "No, what did you say?"

01:02:06.400 --> 01:02:08.062
And he said, "It's good, good, good."

01:02:08.062 --> 01:02:09.240
(Rhett laughs)

01:02:09.240 --> 01:02:11.867
And I said, "That's exactly right."

01:02:13.370 --> 01:02:14.890
So I'll never forget that.

01:02:14.890 --> 01:02:16.800
- Now what happened to the cows?

01:02:16.800 --> 01:02:17.708
- They're fine.

01:02:17.708 --> 01:02:20.260
- What happens when you--
- His brothers and some,

01:02:20.260 --> 01:02:21.900
he had someone else
who helps with the farm

01:02:21.900 --> 01:02:23.730
to help with the cows.
- Was it Ted, Fred, or Ned?

01:02:23.730 --> 01:02:25.703
- I don't know, probably all of them.

01:02:26.802 --> 01:02:29.250
The cows were taken care
of, we got 'em on a plane,

01:02:29.250 --> 01:02:31.560
his daughter picked 'em
up, drove them home,

01:02:31.560 --> 01:02:33.780
they got home that night.

01:02:33.780 --> 01:02:36.179
It was 10 o'clock at night

01:02:36.179 --> 01:02:39.053
after a long day of traveling
but he was doing fine.

01:02:40.530 --> 01:02:42.760
Evidence being right when they got home,

01:02:42.760 --> 01:02:44.320
they took their luggage in the house

01:02:44.320 --> 01:02:46.960
and then Alison, his
daughter told Christy,

01:02:46.960 --> 01:02:48.540
took the luggage in the house

01:02:48.540 --> 01:02:51.240
and then he immediately came back outside,

01:02:51.240 --> 01:02:53.090
grabbed the leaf blower

01:02:53.090 --> 01:02:55.243
and started blowing off his car port.

01:02:56.130 --> 01:02:58.770
And then the next morning
he got up and he said,

01:02:58.770 --> 01:03:00.417
he was going out the door and Mom said,

01:03:00.417 --> 01:03:02.060
"Well where you going?"

01:03:02.060 --> 01:03:05.240
He's like, "I'm gettin' on the
Gator, I'm going out there."

01:03:05.240 --> 01:03:06.807
She said, "Well you can't
go out there without me.

01:03:06.807 --> 01:03:08.037
"I gotta get ready."

01:03:08.900 --> 01:03:09.733
My mom,

01:03:12.071 --> 01:03:14.330
she likes to take her time to get ready.

01:03:14.330 --> 01:03:15.163
- She takes awhile to get ready.

01:03:15.163 --> 01:03:16.630
- Well, she likes to take her time.

01:03:16.630 --> 01:03:17.640
- Well, okay.
- Let's just say

01:03:17.640 --> 01:03:18.790
it's a choice.
- I'm just saying

01:03:18.790 --> 01:03:20.058
I remember it from my childhood.

01:03:20.058 --> 01:03:21.900
(Link chuckles)

01:03:21.900 --> 01:03:23.880
- And he was like, "No,
I'm going out right now."

01:03:23.880 --> 01:03:25.850
And she said, "Well let
me put on my house coat,"

01:03:25.850 --> 01:03:27.487
and then she was telling
Christy, she was like,

01:03:27.487 --> 01:03:30.857
"For the first time ever, here
I am in just my housecoat,

01:03:30.857 --> 01:03:32.047
"driving around out here in the Gator."

01:03:32.047 --> 01:03:33.650
- Can't let the cows see you.

01:03:33.650 --> 01:03:37.190
- And then, so he surveyed
his property, came back,

01:03:37.190 --> 01:03:39.690
parked the Gator, went inside,
sat down in his recliner

01:03:39.690 --> 01:03:43.050
and Mom said that was the
first time he seemed 100%

01:03:43.050 --> 01:03:45.350
at ease in over a week and a half.

01:03:45.350 --> 01:03:47.630
- Yeah, he was back in his environs.

01:03:47.630 --> 01:03:49.340
- Yeah so he's doing well,

01:03:49.340 --> 01:03:53.747
he's got to tackle these ulcers
long-term and figure out,

01:03:53.747 --> 01:03:55.923
and they're gonna monitor
that and they're gonna figure,

01:03:55.923 --> 01:03:57.178
they're gonna get to the bottom of it.

01:03:57.178 --> 01:03:58.780
- They're getting to the
bottom of why it's happening.

01:03:58.780 --> 01:04:01.460
- Right but he didn't
have to stay in California

01:04:01.460 --> 01:04:03.810
to deal with all of that so
he's doing that back home

01:04:03.810 --> 01:04:07.680
and like I said I talked to him
right before we came in here

01:04:07.680 --> 01:04:09.742
and he said he was in a meeting.

01:04:09.742 --> 01:04:11.170
- Yeah.
- With some guys

01:04:11.170 --> 01:04:12.930
and that he was doing something tonight

01:04:12.930 --> 01:04:15.180
